    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s40494-026-02512-9",
    "abstract": "Abstract Climate change poses escalating risks to cultural heritage with substantial implications for adaptation to minimize losses and damages. Integrating expert elicitation with projected changes in mean and extreme temperature and precipitation, we assess climate risk for 148 heritage sites and buffer zones within 39 Cultural World Heritage properties in China. Even under a moderate-emissions pathway (SSP245), risk increases for 82% of sites by mid\u2011century, with all sites reaching moderate to extreme risk by the 2070 s; by century\u2019s end, over one\u2011third shift from low to high, or extreme risk. Ancient Architectural Structures are at risk in the near term, including Mount Wutai and the Yungang Grottoes. Over half of the sites are located in urban or peri\u2011urban areas, with nine urban sites facing extreme risk by 2100 regardless of scenario. These findings underscore the urgent need for adaptation across properties and buffer zones and offer guidance for scheduling site\u2011specific interventions.",
    "field": "Climate change and cultural heritage",
    "problem": "The lack of large-scale, systematic climate risk assessments for China's cultural heritage, which combine diverse climate hazards with site vulnerabilities, hinders the development of urgent and informed adaptation plans to prevent damage.",
    "method": "We propose a climate risk assessment method that integrates expert elicitation via the Analytic Hierarchy Process with projected changes in mean and extreme temperature and precipitation to quantify future risks for different categories of cultural heritage sites in China.",
    "task": "The task is to assess future climate risk for China's Cultural World Heritage properties by combining climate change projections for temperature and precipitation with expert elicitation on the vulnerability of different heritage types to inform adaptation needs.",
